  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct method is to place the <b>cron-linux.config</b> file inside the <code>.ebextensions</code> directory in your application bundle, which allows Elastic Beanstalk to automatically set up cron jobs on all instances during environment provisioning. Creating your environment with the <code>aws elasticbeanstalk create-environment</code> CLI or using the <code>eb cli</code> ensures these configuration files are processed as part of environment setup.</p><p>Option <i>\"Upload the cron-linux file to S3, then reference it using the Beanstalk environment UI without any config files.\"</i> is incorrect because merely uploading to S3 and referencing via UI doesn’t automatically associate the file with instance initialization, nor does it automate cron setup.<br>Option <i>\"Directly add the cron job after environment creation by SSH-ing to each instance manually; no config file is necessary when using the CLI.\"</i> is not scalable or maintainable, as instance replacements would require repeating manual steps.<br>Option <i>\"Place cron-linux.yaml in your application's root folder, and run eb init followed by eb create to deploy the environment.\"</i> is also incorrect, because Beanstalk expects configuration files to be in <code>.ebextensions</code> and with a <code>.config</code> extension rather than <code>.yaml</code>.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> Always use the <code>.ebextensions</code> directory with <code>.config</code> files for environment customizations. This ensures your environment remains consistent, repeatable, and fully automated.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You have been assigned to deploy a PHP web application to AWS Elastic Beanstalk using the CLI, and need to ensure a cron job is configured on all EC2 instances. The configuration file named <code>cron-linux</code> has already been created and uploaded together with your application files in an S3 bucket. Which config file format and CLI approach should you use to properly launch this environment?</p>",
